    neither bottoms out at the add), which is not a real ideal -- it has no frontier head to fold.
    Reasoning about *versions* produced (never picking a single canonical producer op) keeps it
    immune to the after-value collision that the graph-edge form mis-resolves.

    Computed as a single Kahn's-algorithm topological pass, O(ops + edges): each op's unmet-
    prerequisite count (its own before/requires/declared preds, resolved to the op that produces
    each) is tracked as an indegree, and an op is queued the moment that count hits zero. A prereq
    that resolves to no producer, or to an op outside `ideal_ids`, simply never fires -- so ops
    that depend on it (directly or via a cycle) are correctly left ungrounded, same as the
    fixpoint this replaces. Previously a `while changed:` loop rescanning every remaining op each
    pass -- O(ops^2) on a long, mostly-linear chain."""
    ideal_set = set(ideal_ids)
    by_id = {op.id: op for op in ops if op.id in ideal_set}
    declared_preds: dict[str, set[str]] = {}
    for a, b in declared:
        if b in by_id:
            declared_preds.setdefault(b, set()).add(a)

    producer_of: dict[tuple[str, str], str] = {}
    for op_id, op in by_id.items():
        for sym, (_before, after) in op.footprint.items():
            producer_of[(sym, after)] = op_id

    prereqs: dict[str, set[str]] = {}
    for op_id, op in by_id.items():
