% Figure 3. The whole model in one picture: what a save writes, what a version
% is, and where the bytes in your files come from. Same session as Figure 1.
\newcommand{\FigThreeCaption}{The three things a developer has to believe to use
\sgt{}. A save writes one record per changed function and groups the records
under a guess at the request they served, which the developer overrules with one
command (left). A version is the set of those records, and two rules decide
which sets are legal, so removing the price cache is subtracting six records from
eighteen (middle). The files are rebuilt from the set, with the text between
functions kept exactly, so the same set always produces the same bytes (right).
The three functions marked $\triangle$ had been written on top of a cache edit,
so \sgt{} re-drafts them with the cache's contribution taken out and asks the
developer's test suite whether the result stands.}

\newcommand{\FigThreeDescription}{Three panels. The left panel shows a terminal
transcript of one save command, followed by the three feature groups the save
landed in, one of them newly created and unnamed, and then a card listing the
six fields one edit record holds. The middle panel lists the fourteen functions
this session changed, grouped by file, with the edit glyphs for each on a short
rail, in two columns. The left column is the set as it stands and the right
column is the same list after the price cache has been reverted, where the cache
edits are drawn as dotted outlines and three functions are marked with a hollow
triangle. The right panel draws the file api.py twice as a vertical stack of
bands, once before and once after, where coloured bands are function bodies and
grey bands are the text between them, which is identical in both.}

