% Figure 3. The whole model in one picture: what a save writes, what a version
% is, and where the bytes in your files come from. Same session as Figure 1.
\newcommand{\FigThreeCaption}{The three things a developer has to believe to use
\sgt{}. A save writes one record per changed function and files the records under
a guess at the request they served, which the developer overrules with one
command (left). A version is the set of those records, and two rules decide which
sets are legal, so taking out the price cache is subtracting six records from
eighteen (middle). The files are rebuilt from the set with the text between
functions kept exactly, so the same set always produces the same bytes (right).
The three functions marked with a hollow triangle had been written on top of a
cache edit, so \sgt{} re-drafts them with the cache's contribution taken out and
asks the developer's test suite whether the result stands.}

