% Figure 4. Every operation sgt offers, the state change it makes, and its bill.
% Each thumbnail reads left to right: the set before the command, then after.
\newcommand{\FigFourCaption}{The ten operations, each drawn as the set before the
command and the set after it. The rails and marks are the ones in
Figure~\ref{fig:two-records}, and a dotted outline is an edit the version no
longer includes. Nine of the ten take a name a developer would use in a sentence,
which is the whole point of recording at this grain. The right-hand column is
what each one costs, and we have put it here rather than in a limitations section
because a developer meets these costs on the first day.}

\newcommand{\FigFourDescription}{A ten row table. Each row has a small diagram, a
command, a sentence saying what the command does for the developer, and a
sentence saying what it costs. The diagrams reuse the rail and mark vocabulary of
Figure 1, with dotted outlines for edits a version no longer includes.}

\newcommand{\opsrow}[4]{%
  \raisebox{0.2ex}{#1} & \cmd{#2} & #3 & #4 \\[0.7ex]}

{\footnotesize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{2.4mm}
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{14mm}p{40mm}p{51mm}p{50mm}@{}}
\toprule
& \textbf{command} & \textbf{what the developer gets} & \textbf{what it costs} \\
\midrule
\opsrow{\thsave}{sgt save -m "..."}
  {One record per changed function, filed under a name for the request.}
  {The grouping is a guess. When it is wrong the developer moves labels, and no
   recorded edit changes.}
\opsrow{\thwhy}{sgt show api.py::handle}
  {The request this function came from, the saves that touched it, and how many
   edits removing it would take along.}
  {The answer is exactly as good as the grouping behind it.}
\opsrow{\threvert}{sgt revert "price cache"}
  {The named set subtracted from the version, with the resulting diff printed
   before anything is applied.}
  {Functions written on top of the set are re-drafted, and the developer's tests
   are what decides whether the draft stands.}
\opsrow{\threstore}{sgt restore "price cache"}
  {The same set back, byte for byte, with everything added since left alone.}
  {Refused while a competing version of the same function is live. \sgt{} names
   it and offers the swap.}
\opsrow{\thdiff}{sgt diff main feat/export}
  {The edits each version has that the other does not, in both directions.}
  {It answers in functions, so seeing which lines moved still means opening the
   file.}
\opsrow{\thswitch}{sgt switch feat/export}
  {The working files rebuilt from that branch's set.}
  {Refused on unsaved edits, because \sgt{} has nowhere to put them yet.}
\opsrow{\thckpt}{sgt intent list}
