% Figure 3. The whole model in one picture: what a save writes, what a version
% is, and where the bytes in your files come from. Same session as Figure 1.
\newcommand{\FigThreeCaption}{What one save writes down, what a version is, and
where the bytes in a file come from. A save writes one record per changed function
and files it under a guess at the request it served, which the developer corrects
with one command (1). A version is the set of those records, so taking out the
price cache is subtracting six records from eighteen (2). The files are rebuilt
from the set with the text between functions kept exactly (3). The two marks in the
right-hand list are the two consequences \sgt{} reports and does not act on. The
triangle on \protect\fsym{api.py::handle} means the retry policy edited that
function after the cache did, so \sgt{} took the cache's lines out of the current
text and the developer's test suite decides whether the result stands. The circles
are three functions that still call \protect\fsym{cache.py::get\_cached}.}

